# Feature-Space Dissimilarity Pseudo-Absence Strategy

## Background

The current pseudo-absence strategy selects random points ≥3,000 m from known VMS deposits (geographic/Euclidean exclusion). Parsa & Cumani (2025) demonstrate that **geospatially dissimilar** negative labels — selected to be maximally dissimilar to deposits in **multi-dimensional feature space**, not just geographic space — produce stronger classifier discrimination and improved exploration targeting efficiency.

The limitation of pure geographic buffers is that proximity in space does not guarantee similarity in geological character. Two points equidistant from a deposit may have completely different geophysical/geochemical signatures — one may look geologically identical to a deposit while the other looks nothing like one. The feature-space approach selects negatives that are most unlike deposits in their **actual geological fingerprint**.

---

## Proposed Approach: Mahalanobis Distance Dissimilarity Sampling

Rather than a geographic exclusion buffer, pseudo-absences will be selected using **Mahalanobis distance in normalized feature space** from the centroid of the positive (deposit) class. Points with the **highest Mahalanobis distance** (i.e., most dissimilar to the deposit cluster in geophysical/geochemical space) are selected as pseudo-negatives.

### Why Mahalanobis Distance?
- Unlike Euclidean distance in feature space, Mahalanobis distance accounts for **feature correlations and variances**, preventing high-variance features from dominating the dissimilarity measure.
- It defines an ellipsoidal "deposit envelope" in feature space, and selects pseudo-negatives outside and far from that envelope.
- Consistent with the spirit of Parsa & Cumani (2025): negatives in maximally different geological environments from the deposits.

### Workflow

```
All unlabelled grid points (full feature matrix)
        ↓
Sample geophysical raster values (already done by extract_features.py)
        ↓
Compute Mahalanobis distance of each candidate point to the VMS deposit centroid in feature space
        ↓
Rank candidates by dissimilarity (highest = most unlike deposits)
        ↓
Sample n=125 pseudo-absences from the top dissimilarity quantile (e.g., top 40%)
        ↓  (stratified to ensure spatial spread across the footprint)
Combine with 125 confirmed barren holes → 250 negatives
```

A **secondary minimum geographic buffer** of 1,000 m (reduced from 3,000 m) is retained as a hard constraint to prevent accidentally labelling points immediately adjacent to known deposits, while the primary selection driver is feature-space dissimilarity.

